--- body ---
## Context

IDH's safety intent is durable — no destructive broad deletion, no commit on main, no merge
from the wrong worktree, no credential disclosure — while Claude permission matchers and
Codex rules are implementation details. Today those levels are mixed in prose, JSON and
shell scripts.

## Relevant files

- `settings.shared.json` — Claude permission syntax
- `rules/git.md` and `rules/workflow.md` — normative safety intent
- `scripts/guard-destructive-bash.sh`, `scripts/guard-commit-on-main.sh`
- `scripts/guard-worktree-identity.sh` and related tests

## Actions

1. Create a portable policy catalog with stable IDs, rationale, severity and required
   enforcement level.
2. Map each policy to deterministic shell guard, provider-native permission rule, agent
   instruction, or a documented combination.
3. Distinguish preventive enforcement from advisory guidance and post-hoc verification.
4. Generate provider configuration without embedding personal paths or credentials.
5. Refuse adapter activation when a mandatory policy lacks an effective enforcement path.

## Test

- Run a table of allowed and forbidden command fixtures through the portable policy runner
  and both provider renderers; mutation-test at least the main-commit and destructive-path
  guards.

## Verification

- [ ] Every existing guard and permission entry maps to a policy or is removed with reason
- [ ] Most-restrictive resolution wins when policies overlap
- [ ] Generated configs contain no secret values
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Portability cannot weaken a destructive-action or credential boundary.
- Provider permission syntax supplements deterministic guards; it is not evidence by itself.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Portable policy catalog and mappings implemented
- [ ] Claude behavior preserved for representative safety cases
- [ ] Codex gaps are explicit and block unsafe workflows
